  </p>

  <h3>Where Round Profile Pictures Are Used</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Messaging Platforms:</strong> WhatsApp, Telegram, Signal, Facebook Messenger, iMessage, and WeChat.</li>
    <li><strong>Social Media Networks:</strong> Instagram, Facebook, Twitter/X, TikTok, Pinterest, and Threads.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional & Business Networks:</strong> LinkedIn, GitHub, Slack, Microsoft Teams, Zoom, and Google Workspace (Gmail).</li>
    <li><strong>Creative & Developer Portfolios:</strong> Behance, Dribbble, Medium, Substack, and YouTube channel avatars.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Design Psychology of the Circle</h3>
  <p>
    User interface (UI) designers prioritize circular avatars for several scientific and visual reasons:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Facial Symmetry Focus:</strong> The human face is inherently curved and organic. A circular frame naturally directs viewer gaze toward the eyes and mouth, eliminating distracting corner backgrounds.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual Hierarchy & Differentiation:</strong> Most content on the web (photos, videos, articles, and cards) is rectangular. Making user avatars circular creates an instant visual distinction between <em>the creator</em> (the person) and <em>the content</em> (the post).</li>
    <li><strong>Interface Softness:</strong> Rounded elements feel friendly, approachable, and modern, reducing visual clutter in dense chat feeds and notification sidebars.</li>
  </ol>

